t**: Affected code, APIs, dependencies, or systems.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: The Capabilities section is critical. It creates the contract between\\nproposal and specs phases. Research existing specs before filling this in:\\nrun `openspec list --specs` for the project's capability inventory, then\\n`openspec show \\\"<spec-id>\\\" --type spec --json --no-scenarios` for any that\\nlook related - that returns a capability's purpose and requirement texts\\nwithout pulling whole spec files into context. Append `--store \\\"<id>\\\"` to\\nboth commands only for a registered standalone store, and keep `--type\\nspec`: a change and a spec sharing a name is otherwise an ambiguous-item\\nerror. `openspec list` without `--specs` lists in-flight changes, not\\nspecs - it never shows what the project already covers. Reuse an existing\\ncapability's exact path instead of introducing a near-duplicate name.\\nThe filtered read is only an overview. Before deciding what is already\\ncovered or what should change, read each relevant spec in full, including\\nscenarios, with `openspec show \\\"<spec-id>\\\" --type spec` (same `--store` rule).\\nEach capability listed here will need a corresponding spec file.\\n\\nEvery change must either declare at least one capability (new or\\nmodified) or explicitly opt out of specs: `openspec validate` rejects a\\nchange with zero deltas unless the change's `.openspec.yaml` sets\\n`skip_specs: true`. Use `skip_specs: true` only when no spec-level\\nbehavior changes (pure refactor, tooling, docs) - specs describe\\nbehavior, so if behavior does not change, no spec should change either.\\nDo not invent a requirement just to satisfy validation.\\n\\nKeep it concise (1-2 pages). Focus on the \\\"why\\\" not the \\\"how\\\" -\\nimplementation details belong in design.md.\\n\\nThis is the foundation - specs, design, and tasks all build on this.\\n\",\n  \"template\": \"# Proposal\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n<!-- Explain the motivation for this change. What problem does this solve? Why now? -->\\n\\n## What Changes\\n\\n<!-- Describe what will change. Be specific about new capabilities, modifications, or removals. -->\\n\\n## Capabilities\\n\\n### New Capabilities\\n<!-- Capabilities being introduced. Use kebab-case for path segments you introduce\\n     (e.g., user-auth or identity/user-auth) that follow the project's existing\\n     spec organization. Each creates specs/<capability-path>/spec.md. -->\\n- `<capability-path>`: <brief description of what this capability covers>\\n\\n### Modified Capabilities\\n<!-- Existing capabilities whose REQUIREMENTS are changing (not just implementation).\\n     Only list here if spec-level behavior changes. Each needs a delta spec file.\\n     Use the exact existing path under openspec/specs/. Leave empty if no requirement\\n     changes. A change with no capabilities at all (pure refactor, tooling, docs)\\n     must set `skip_specs: true` in its .openspec.yaml - openspec validate rejects\\n     a zero-delta change without that marker. Do not invent a requirement just to\\n     satisfy validation. -->\\n- `<existing-capability-path>`: <what requirement is changing>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<!-- Affected code, APIs, dependencies, systems -->\\n\",\n  \"dependencies\": [],\n  \"unlocks\": [\n    \"specs\",\n    \"design\"\n  ],\n  \"root\": {\n    \"path\": \"/workspace\",\n    \"source\": \"nearest\"\n  }\n}\nsrc/flask/sansio/app.py\nsrc/flask/app.py\ntests/test_apps/cliapp/app.py\ntests/test_apps/cliapp/importerrorapp.py\ntests/test_apps/cliapp/multiapp.py\ntests/test_basic.py\nsrc/flask/testing.py:36:        :data:`SERVER_NAME`, and :data:`APPLICATION_ROOT`.\nsrc/flask/testing.py:37:    :param subdomain: Subdomain name to append to :data:`SERVER_NAME`.\nsrc/flask/testing.py:66:            http_host = app.config.get(\"SERVER_NAME\") or \"localhost\"\nsrc/flask/sansio/app.py:123:       :data:`SERVER_NAME` does not implicitly enable it.\nsrc/flask/sansio/app.py:138:        :data:`SERVER_NAME` when matching routes. Defaults to False.\nsrc/flask/app.py:173:       :data:`SERVER_NAME` does not implicitly enable it.\nsrc/flask/app.py:188:        :data:`SERVER_NAME` when matching routes. Defaults to False.\nsrc/flask/app.py:216:            \"SERVER_NAME\": None,\nsrc/flask/app.py:515:            If :data:`SERVER_NAME` is set, it does not restrict requests to\nsrc/flask/app.py:520:            :data:`SERVER_NAME` no longer implicitly enables subdomain\nsrc/flask/app.py:536:            server_name = self.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"]\nsrc/flask/app.py:539:                # Don't pass SERVER_NAME, otherwise it's used and the actual\nsrc/flask/app.py:541:                server_name = None\nsrc/flask/app.py:549:                request.environ, server_name=server_name, subdomain=subdomain\nsrc/flask/app.py:552:        # Need at least SERVER_NAME to match/build outside a request.\nsrc/flask/app.py:553:        if self.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] is not None:\nsrc/flask/app.py:555:                self.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"],\nsrc/flask/app.py:632:    def run(\nsrc/flask/app.py:670:            ``'127.0.0.1'`` or the host in the ``SERVER_NAME`` config variable\nsrc/flask/app.py:673:            port defined in the ``SERVER_NAME`` config variable if present.\nsrc/flask/app.py:692:            The default port is now picked from the ``SERVER_NAME``\nsrc/flask/app.py:720:        server_name = self.config.get(\"SERVER_NAME\")\nsrc/flask/app.py:723:        if server_name:\nsrc/flask/app.py:724:            sn_host, _, sn_port = server_name.partition(\":\")\nsrc/flask/app.py:1127:        this requires setting :data:`SERVER_NAME` so Flask knows what\nsrc/flask/app.py:1187:                    \" without 'SERVER_NAME' configured. Also configure\"\nsrc/flask/app.py:1541:            :data:`PREFERRED_URL_SCHEME`, ``subdomain``, :data:`SERVER_NAME`,\nsrc/flask/app.py:1543:        :param subdomain: Subdomain name to prepend to :data:`SERVER_NAME`.\nsrc/flask/sessions.py:183:            Not set by default, does not fall back to ``SERVER_NAME``.\ntests/test_basic.py:312:        SERVER_NAME=\"www.example.com:8080\",\ntests/test_basic.py:1526:def test_server_name_matching(\ntests/test_basic.py:1538:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"example.test\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1562:def test_server_name_subdomain():\ntests/test_basic.py:1574:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"dev.local:5000\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1584:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"dev.local:443\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1591:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"dev.local\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1782:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"localhost.localdomain\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1803:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"localhost.localdomain\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1815:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"localhost.localdomain:3000\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1829:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"localhost.localdomain:3000\"\ntests/test_basic.py:1904:    \"host,port,server_name,expect_host,expect_port\",\ntests/test_basic.py:1916:    monkeypatch, host, port, server_name, expect_host, expect_port, app\ntests/test_basic.py:1923:    app.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = server_name\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
